['Here's a simple implementation of `atoi` in C, along with usage example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int atoi(const char *str) {
    int num = 0;
    int sign = 1;

    if (*str == '-') {
        sign = -1;
        str++;
    } else if (*str == '+') {
        str++;
    }

    for (; *str; str++) {
        num = num * 10 + (*str - '0');
    }
    return sign * num;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
        printf("argv[%d] = %s\n", i, argv[i]);
        printf("atoi(argv[%d]) = %d\n", i, atoi(argv[i]));
        printf("\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, `atoi` function takes a string as an argument and returns the integer value of that string. It handles both positive and negative numbers by checking for `-` or `+` characters at the beginning of the string.

The `main` function demonstrates the usage of `atoi` with command-line arguments. The `for` loop iterates over all command-line arguments (except the first one, which is the program name), prints each argument and its integer value using `atoi`.']

