['Here's an example of how you could implement this using C programming:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <arg>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    // Get the argument from the command line
    const char* arg = argv[1];

    // Find the last non-whitespace character in the string
    int length = strlen(arg);
    for (int i = length - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
        if (!isspace(arg[i])) {
            break;
        }
    }

    // If there were no non-whitespace characters, set the length to 0
    if (i < 0) {
        length = 0;
    } else {
        // Otherwise, the length is the last non-whitespace character + 1
        ++length;
    }

    // Create a new string that contains the trimmed argument
    char* trimmed_arg = malloc((length + 1) * sizeof(char));
    strncpy(trimmed_arg, arg, length);
    trimmed_arg[length] = '\0';

    // Print out the trimmed argument
    printf("The command line argument is: %s\n", trimmed_arg);

    // Free the memory we allocated for the trimmed argument
    free(trimmed_arg);

    return 0;
}
```

This C program will take in a command line argument and print it out to the console. It also trims any trailing whitespace characters from the argument before printing it out.

In this code, we first check if there is exactly one command line argument (i.e., the script itself). If not, we print an error message and exit. Then we use `strlen` to find the length of the argument string. We then iterate backwards over the string until we find a non-whitespace character or the start of the string. This character will be the last non-trailing whitespace character in the string.

We create a new string that contains this trimmed argument by calling `strncpy` to copy the characters up to (but not including) the trailing whitespace into our new string, and then append a null character (`\0`) at the end of the string. Finally, we print out the trimmed argument and free the memory we allocated for it.']

